R-UM1 R-AL4 R-AL5 R-AL6 R-PH13 R-P5 R-PP4 R-OUT2 (slice 036b2): the used-module scan in the oracle loop (`PickLoop.run!`, `Oracle.step_probed`, the verdict `Scan`) with the fake probes. 5 runs of `update` on 5 projects. The packages and the index are those of `probe/un1-units`. acme/pf 0.1.0 is an anchor (`=`). parser 1.0.0 is an anchor too, except in runs 1, 3 and 4 (parser is free: its newest candidate is 1.2.0, then 1.1.0). json is free: its candidates are 2.1.0, 2.0.5 (2.2.0 is too young, so it is only for a named version, run 4). Each run has its own compiler id (a fact is for a pair (hash, compiler), R-P4). `fx/units` has the answers of the unit probes (no row: it passes). `fx/probes` has the answers of the baseline, check and build probes (the rows are used in order over the whole case; a run with no row passes). `research/slice-036b2-revert/gen-sc1.py` makes the case.
The calls of the scan, in order: the unit probes of the round, `db_write` with the facts, then the scan of the tuple that the step chose: one `read_files` for each level of the used modules (`UnitFlow.scan_in!`, no read when the app imports nothing of the package), then the solver step runs again. A unit fail that the scan finds in a used module drops that release only (a conflict of the solver with a probe cause, R-AL5): the pair of the next release is asked for, and the ladder runs once, on the new tuple. The ladder does not drop a tuple of the loop (no line `note: tuple dropped`).
Run 1 (the named blame of the review of slice 036b, B10): `update json parser`, both are free, and the app imports `json.Yaml`. The first tuple is json 2.1.0 with parser 1.2.0, and the 2 unit probes run in one `probes 2` call. The unit check of json 2.1.0 fails in `Yaml.roc`. The scan reads `Yaml.roc` of json 2.1.0 and then `Wire.roc` (`Yaml.roc` imports `Wire`): the fail touches a used module. Only json 2.1.0 is dropped: parser 1.2.0 stays, and its fact is known, so the loop probes json 2.0.5 only (a conflict on the whole tuple would try json 2.1.0 with parser 1.1.0 first). The header gets json 2.0.5 and parser 1.2.0.
Run 2 (R-UM1, `main.roc`): `update json`, and the app imports `json.Yaml`. The unit check of json 2.1.0 fails in `main.roc` of the package. Roc reads `main.roc` of each package of the closure, so this fail touches the app with no scan: no file of json 2.1.0 is read, the release is dropped at once, and the header gets json 2.0.5. (A scan here would read `Yaml.roc` of json 2.1.0.)
Run 3 (the scan result is kept): `update json parser`, and the app imports `parser.Parser`. The unit check of parser 1.2.0 fails in `Yaml.roc`: the scan reads `Parser.roc` of parser 1.2.0 once, finds that the app does not use `Yaml.roc`, and keeps the row (also with no used module in it), so the fail is no blame and the tuple stays (R-UM1). The whole-app check has a new error in acme/json 2.1.0 (the baseline has the error of parser 1.0.0, so that one is not new): the ladder drops json 2.1.0 (named blame, the line `note: tuple dropped: ...`). The next tuple has json 2.0.5 and parser 1.2.0 again: the loop does not scan parser 1.2.0 a second time (the log has one scan of the loop; the ladder reads `Parser.roc` of its own in each of its 2 runs, as it did before this part). The summary has the note that module Yaml fails and the app does not import it.
Run 4 (Q7, the lines before the ladder print once): `update json@2.2.0 parser`. json 2.2.0 is one day old: the version is named, so it is chosen, and the line `note: json 2.2.0 is 1d old (min-release-age 2d)` is printed before the ladder. The check has a new error in acme/parser 1.2.0: the ladder drops parser 1.2.0 (named blame). The next tuple has json 2.2.0 and parser 1.1.0, and its note is the same text. The command prints a line before the ladder once: the stdout has the note `json 2.2.0 is 1d old` one time, then `note: tuple dropped: ...`.
Run 5 (gap test, mutation A9 of the code agent: one `Scan` call has 2 releases): `update json parser`, and the app imports `json.Yaml`. Both unit checks fail in a module with the same name, `Yaml.roc`: json 2.1.0 and parser 1.2.0 (one `probes 2` call). The step asks one scan for both releases. The app uses `Yaml.roc` of json only, so only json 2.1.0 touches a used module: it is dropped, and parser 1.2.0 stays (its fail is no blame). The loop probes json 2.0.5 only. If the used modules of all scanned releases went to each release, parser 1.2.0 would be dropped too and the tuple would have parser 1.1.0. The summary has the note that module Yaml fails (parser 1.2.0) and the app does not import it.
